:- module(is_evaluable_host_term, [go/2, user_value/2]).
:- use_module(library(prelude)).

% Regression test: `is` on a *bound* compound evaluates it when the
% compound's functor names a declared function or a host call. A
% `host:` call in data position stores the vmName `host__F` as its
% functor, which the host-call registry (keyed by the bare name `F`)
% did not match, so `R is X` reported `is: functor is not evaluable:
% host__+/2` on both backends. The deep evaluator now decodes the
% functor before the host-call fallback, and both backends agree.
%
% The rule bodies evaluate the argument through the `is`-on-a-variable
% path (`SEvalIs` / `deep-eval-value`), which is the path under test:
% a bare `R is host:'+'(1, 2)` would be compiled as a direct host call
% and never reach the walker.
%
% The argument-shaped cases cover a declared call's arguments: one is
% dispatched when its own functor is evaluable (a nested host call, a
% prelude operation) and a constructor argument stays data. The
% discriminating case for the argument-walk fix is the list in
% test/golden/is_evaluable_host_term_args/, whose cons cell the old
% walker dispatched before checking the enclosing `member/2`.
%
% The `user_value` case pins the first tier: a declared function's
% functor is qualified (`is_evaluable_host_term__user_value`), so the
% evaluables table answers it and no host-registry tier is consulted.
% Dropping that table lookup fails the case; the host tiers cannot
% match the qualified functor, so reordering alone would not.

:- chr_constraint go(any, any).

go(X, R) <=> R is X.

:- function user_value/2.

% A user function at the arity of the prelude addition. Its own
% arguments are ignored, so the result distinguishes it from the host
% call it stands in for.
user_value(_, _) -> 99.
